analysis:
Both units are historical CHANGELOG.md text (head lines 377-742, v0.10.8, v0.10.7 and earlier release notes) and contain no executable code. The only changed hunk in this file (head lines 11-39) adds a v0.10.10 candidate header and waitlist notes. Those lines are outside the supplied units and appear only in a truncated patch excerpt.

The supplied text describes security-relevant provider behavior from earlier releases that I could not check against source:
- cluster gpu-limit, which relates to TB-014, A-020 and T-062;
- sandbox setup with --disk, which relates to TB-013, A-019 and T-061;
- uninstall and the update, rollback and watchdog flows, which relate to T-043;
- the prefix SSD cache, which relates to T-041.

The changelog does not state any change to the SSD cache TTL, the 30-minute bound or the HMAC lookup names, so the T-041 mitigations appear unaffected.

No security-control change or new attack surface is demonstrated by these units. Documentation drift against the real code cannot be verified here.

The new v0.10.10 waitlist gating (a new sandbox or a new pair needs account approval; existing setups are exempt; the 'waitlists not offered yet' fallback proceeds as before) is a policy and authorization control. It is not visible in these units. If the code implementing it ships in this PR, the next pass should check three things:
- the fail-open or fallback path when the coordinator does not offer waitlists;
- whether the approval is enforced server-side or only by the CLI;
- the existing-setup exemption for bypasses.

The truncated patch excerpt prevents confirming this.
